# Sharded Concurrent Map in Rust

The idea: split the map into N independent shards, each guarded by its own `RwLock<HashMap<...>>`. A key's shard is chosen by hashing, so unrelated keys rarely contend. Reads are concurrent within a shard (RwLock), and writes only block one shard at a time.

```rust
use std::borrow::Borrow;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::collections::hash_map::RandomState;
use std::hash::{BuildHasher, Hash, Hasher};
use std::sync::RwLock;

/// A thread-safe map sharded across multiple `RwLock`-protected `HashMap`s.
///
/// Keys are routed to a shard by hash, so independent keys generally do not
/// contend. Reads on a single shard run concurrently; writes on a shard are
/// exclusive but do not block other shards.
pub struct ConcurrentMap<K, V, S = RandomState> {
    shards: Box<[RwLock<HashMap<K, V, S>>]>,
    hasher: S,
    /// `shard_count - 1`, used as a bitmask. `shard_count` is a power of two.
    shard_mask: usize,
}

impl<K, V> ConcurrentMap<K, V, RandomState>
where
    K: Hash + Eq,
{
    /// Create a map with a sensible default shard count.
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self::with_shards(default_shard_count())
    }

    /// Create a map with the given shard count (rounded up to a power of two).
    pub fn with_shards(shard_count: usize) -> Self {
        Self::with_shards_and_hasher(shard_count, RandomState::new())
    }
}

impl<K, V> Default for ConcurrentMap<K, V, RandomState>
where
    K: Hash + Eq,
{
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl<K, V, S> ConcurrentMap<K, V, S>
where
    K: Hash + Eq,
    S: BuildHasher + Clone,
{
    /// Create a map with a custom hasher and shard count.
    pub fn with_shards_and_hasher(shard_count: usize, hasher: S) -> Self {
        let shard_count = shard_count.max(1).next_power_of_two();
        let shards: Vec<_> = (0..shard_count)
            .map(|_| RwLock::new(HashMap::with_hasher(hasher.clone())))
            .collect();
        Self {
            shards: shards.into_boxed_slice(),
            hasher,
            shard_mask: shard_count - 1,
        }
    }

    /// Number of shards.
    pub fn shard_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.shards.len()
    }

    #[inline]
    fn shard_index<Q>(&self, key: &Q) -> usize
    where
        Q: Hash + ?Sized,
    {
        let mut h = self.hasher.build_hasher();
        key.hash(&mut h);
        (h.finish() as usize) & self.shard_mask
    }

    /// Insert a value, returning the previous value if any.
    pub fn insert(&self, key: K, value: V) -> Option<V> {
        let idx = self.shard_index(&key);
        self.shards[idx].write().expect("shard poisoned").insert(key, value)
    }

    /// Remove a value, returning it if it was present.
    pub fn remove<Q>(&self, key: &Q) -> Option<V>
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(key);
        self.shards[idx].write().expect("shard poisoned").remove(key)
    }

    /// Returns true if the map contains the key.
    pub fn contains_key<Q>(&self, key: &Q) -> bool
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(key);
        self.shards[idx].read().expect("shard poisoned").contains_key(key)
    }

    /// Get a clone of the value for the key.
    ///
    /// Use [`with`](Self::with) when `V: Clone` is too expensive or unavailable.
    pub fn get<Q>(&self, key: &Q) -> Option<V>
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
        V: Clone,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(key);
        self.shards[idx].read().expect("shard poisoned").get(key).cloned()
    }

    /// Read-access to a value by key, scoped to a closure.
    ///
    /// The shard read-lock is held only while `f` runs; do not perform other
    /// operations on the same map inside `f` (especially writes to the same
    /// shard) or you may deadlock.
    pub fn with<Q, F, R>(&self, key: &Q, f: F) -> Option<R>
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
        F: FnOnce(&V) -> R,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(key);
        self.shards[idx].read().expect("shard poisoned").get(key).map(f)
    }

    /// Mutable access to a value by key, scoped to a closure.
    ///
    /// The shard write-lock is held only while `f` runs; the same caveat as
    /// [`with`](Self::with) applies.
    pub fn with_mut<Q, F, R>(&self, key: &Q, f: F) -> Option<R>
    where
        K: Borrow<Q>,
        Q: Hash + Eq + ?Sized,
        F: FnOnce(&mut V) -> R,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(key);
        self.shards[idx].write().expect("shard poisoned").get_mut(key).map(f)
    }

    /// Get-or-insert with a default factory, returning a clone of the value.
    pub fn get_or_insert_with<F>(&self, key: K, default: F) -> V
    where
        F: FnOnce() -> V,
        V: Clone,
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(&key);
        let mut shard = self.shards[idx].write().expect("shard poisoned");
        shard.entry(key).or_insert_with(default).clone()
    }

    /// Atomically update an existing entry or insert a new one.
    pub fn upsert<F>(&self, key: K, default: V, update: F)
    where
        F: FnOnce(&mut V),
    {
        let idx = self.shard_index(&key);
        let mut shard = self.shards[idx].write().expect("shard poisoned");
        shard.entry(key).and_modify(|v| update(v)).or_insert(default);
    }

    /// Total number of entries across all shards.
    ///
    /// Note: this is a snapshot summed shard-by-shard; concurrent writers may
    /// change the total while this runs.
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.shards
            .iter()
            .map(|s| s.read().expect("shard poisoned").len())
            .sum()
    }

    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.shards
            .iter()
            .all(|s| s.read().expect("shard poisoned").is_empty())
    }

    pub fn clear(&self) {
        for shard in self.shards.iter() {
            shard.write().expect("shard poisoned").clear();
        }
    }

    /// Iterate over all entries, holding each shard's read-lock during its pass.
    pub fn for_each<F>(&self, mut f: F)
    where
        F: FnMut(&K, &V),
    {
        for shard in self.shards.iter() {
            let guard = shard.read().expect("shard poisoned");
            for (k, v) in guard.iter() {
                f(k, v);
            }
        }
    }

    /// Retain only entries for which the predicate returns true.
    pub fn retain<F>(&self, mut f: F)
    where
        F: FnMut(&K, &mut V) -> bool,
    {
        for shard in self.shards.iter() {
            shard
                .write()
                .expect("shard poisoned")
                .retain(|k, v| f(k, v));
        }
    }
}

fn default_shard_count() -> usize {
    let cpus = std::thread::available_parallelism()
        .map(|n| n.get())
        .unwrap_or(4);
    // 4x CPU count gives headroom for write contention; clamp to a sane range.
    (cpus * 4).next_power_of_two().clamp(16, 1024)
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use std::sync::Arc;
    use std::thread;

    #[test]
    fn basic_insert_get_remove() {
        let map: ConcurrentMap<String, i32> = ConcurrentMap::new();
        assert!(map.is_empty());

        assert_eq!(map.insert("a".into(), 1), None);
        assert_eq!(map.insert("a".into(), 2), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(map.get("a"), Some(2));
        assert!(map.contains_key("a"));
        assert_eq!(map.remove("a"), Some(2));
        assert_eq!(map.get("a"), None);
    }

    #[test]
    fn closure_access() {
        let map: ConcurrentMap<&'static str, Vec<u32>> = ConcurrentMap::new();
        map.insert("xs", vec![1, 2, 3]);

        let sum = map.with("xs", |v| v.iter().sum::<u32>());
        assert_eq!(sum, Some(6));

        map.with_mut("xs", |v| v.push(4));
        assert_eq!(map.get("xs").map(|v| v.len()), Some(4));
    }

    #[test]
    fn upsert_counts() {
        let map: ConcurrentMap<&'static str, u64> = ConcurrentMap::new();
        for _ in 0..5 {
            map.upsert("hits", 1, |v| *v += 1);
        }
        assert_eq!(map.get("hits"), Some(5));
    }

    #[test]
    fn concurrent_inserts() {
        let map = Arc::new(ConcurrentMap::<u64, u64>::with_shards(64));
        let threads = 8;
        let per_thread = 10_000u64;

        let handles: Vec<_> = (0..threads)
            .map(|t| {
                let m = Arc::clone(&map);
                thread::spawn(move || {
                    let base = t * per_thread;
                    for i in 0..per_thread {
                        m.insert(base + i, base + i);
                    }
                })
            })
            .collect();

        for h in handles {
            h.join().unwrap();
        }

        assert_eq!(map.len(), (threads * per_thread) as usize);
        for t in 0..threads {
            for i in [0, per_thread / 2, per_thread - 1] {
                let k = t * per_thread + i;
                assert_eq!(map.get(&k), Some(k));
            }
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn concurrent_counter_via_upsert() {
        let map = Arc::new(ConcurrentMap::<&'static str, u64>::new());
        let threads = 16;
        let per_thread = 5_000;

        let handles: Vec<_> = (0..threads)
            .map(|_| {
                let m = Arc::clone(&map);
                thread::spawn(move || {
                    for _ in 0..per_thread {
                        m.upsert("n", 1, |v| *v += 1);
                    }
                })
            })
            .collect();
        for h in handles {
            h.join().unwrap();
        }

        assert_eq!(map.get("n"), Some((threads * per_thread) as u64));
    }
}
```

## Design notes

- **Why RwLock over Mutex.** Most workloads are read-heavy. `RwLock` lets readers in a shard proceed in parallel; with sufficient shards, writers also rarely block each other. If your workload is overwhelmingly writes, swap `RwLock` for `parking_lot::Mutex` (less overhead) by changing two type names and the `read()`/`write()` calls.
- **Shard count is a power of two.** That lets shard selection use a bitmask (`hash & (n-1)`) instead of a modulo, which is meaningfully faster and produces a uniform distribution as long as the hasher is good (which `RandomState` is).
- **Default shard count.** `(cpus * 4)` rounded up to a power of two, clamped to `[16, 1024]`. The 4x factor provides headroom so unrelated writers seldom collide on the same shard; the lower bound keeps the structure useful on 1-2 core machines, the upper bound keeps `len()`/`clear()` cheap.
- **Borrow-based lookups.** `get`/`remove`/`contains_key` accept any `&Q` where `K: Borrow<Q>`, so `ConcurrentMap<String, _>` can be queried with `&str`. The `Hash` invariant guarantees borrowed and owned forms route to the same shard.
- **API for non-`Clone` values.** `get` requires `V: Clone`. For values that aren't cheap to clone, use `with`/`with_mut`, which give scoped reference access under the lock. The doc comments warn about reentrant calls, which would deadlock the same shard.
- **`upsert` is the atomic pattern.** Don't compose `get` then `insert`, you'll race. `upsert` and `get_or_insert_with` hold one write-lock for the whole read-modify-write.
- **Poisoning.** I `expect("shard poisoned")` on lock results. A poisoned shard means a panic occurred mid-update on that shard, which signals real corruption; propagating the panic is the safer default. If you'd rather recover, replace with `.unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner())`.
- **Iteration is best-effort.** `for_each`, `len`, `clear` walk shards one at a time, so they aren't a globally-consistent snapshot. That's the standard tradeoff for sharded maps; a globally-consistent snapshot would require locking every shard at once and defeat the point.

If you want to drop the closure-based API in favor of returning guards (à la `dashmap::Ref`), that requires a self-referential guard type holding the `RwLockReadGuard` plus a reference into it, which is implementable with a small amount of unsafe or a crate like `owning_ref`. Happy to sketch that variant if you'd prefer it.
